WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.760 --> 00:00:04.290
Ok so promethium is actually my least favourite
lanthanide because I

00:00:04.290 --> 00:00:10.000
can’t actually work with it. It’s highly
radioactive and at any one time on

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:14.030
the earth’s surface there is not an awful
lot of it so you can’t physically

00:00:14.030 --> 00:00:16.090
get enough of it to do any chemistry with.

00:00:16.090 --> 00:00:22.130
So promethium is the only one of the rare
earth elements that is

00:00:22.130 --> 00:00:25.810
radioactive, but because it’s radioactive
and the atoms decay away it’s

00:00:25.810 --> 00:00:32.140
not found in nature. So it was only after
the start of nuclear research

00:00:32.140 --> 00:00:37.829
that people discovered promethium, even some
of the products from the

00:00:37.829 --> 00:00:42.859
decay from other radioactive elements. And
you can see this quite well:

00:00:42.859 --> 00:00:48.260
this is an old chemistry book that I’ve
got, written by Aston, the famous

00:00:48.260 --> 00:00:53.479
scientist who discovered isotopes – atoms
of the same element that have

00:00:53.479 --> 00:00:58.980
different weights - and in this book which
he published in 1922…

00:00:58.980 --> 00:01:02.499
Hold it a bit more vertical. Ok go ahead.

00:01:02.499 --> 00:01:09.580
…he had a periodic table and if you look
down here, here are the rare

00:01:09.580 --> 00:01:15.390
earths and in this position here there is
a space for element 61,

00:01:15.390 --> 00:01:22.680
promethium. So element 61 wasn’t known in
1922 and it was found

00:01:22.680 --> 00:01:26.780
much later. But you can see the rest of the
elements were, except

00:01:26.780 --> 00:01:30.610
there’s a gap down here under tungsten because,
again, the element

00:01:30.610 --> 00:01:34.970
rhenium was only discovered relatively recently
compared to most of the

00:01:34.970 --> 00:01:35.820
other elements.

